WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.327
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:09.350
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:15.855
Unit twenty-four: Presenting Research. Today you will put key information in focus with 'What … is …'.

4
00:00:16.155 --> 00:00:18.726
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.016
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:24.967
Presentation.

7
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:30.224
Presentation means a structured talk delivering information to an audience.

8
00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:33.455
The presentation was limited to twelve minutes.

9
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.062
Your turn. Say: Presentation.

10
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:40.971
Presentation.

11
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:42.648
Audience.

12
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.764
Audience means the people listening to or watching a talk.

13
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.849
Adapt your terminology to your audience.

14
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.137
Your turn. Say: Audience.

15
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.152
Audience.

16
00:00:57.917 --> 00:00:58.790
Slide.

17
00:00:59.783 --> 00:01:03.211
Slide means a single page of a visual presentation.

18
00:01:03.817 --> 00:01:06.292
Each slide should convey one main idea.

19
00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:09.315
Your turn. Say: Slide.

20
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:13.261
Slide.

21
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:15.134
Key finding.

22
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.009
Key finding means the most important result of a study.

23
00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:22.081
State your key finding within the first minute.

24
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:25.336
Your turn. Say: Key finding.

25
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.445
Key finding.

26
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:30.964
Clarity.

27
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.169
Clarity means the quality of being easy to understand.

28
00:01:35.783 --> 00:01:37.922
Clarity matters more than complexity.

29
00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:41.622
Your turn. Say: Clarity.

30
00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:45.410
Clarity.

31
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.163
Q&A.

32
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.450
Q&A means a session where the audience asks questions.

33
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.572
The Q&A revealed a weakness in the argument.

34
00:01:55.183 --> 00:01:57.766
Your turn. Say: Q&A.

35
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.659
Q&A.

36
00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:03.255
Visual.

37
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.852
Visual means an image, chart or diagram used to communicate.

38
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.576
A strong visual can replace a paragraph of text.

39
00:02:12.183 --> 00:02:14.255
Your turn. Say: Visual.

40
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.031
Visual.

41
00:02:18.783 --> 00:02:19.901
Engagement.

42
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:24.588
Engagement means the degree of attention and involvement of an audience.

43
00:02:25.183 --> 00:02:27.612
Rhetorical questions increase engagement.

44
00:02:28.217 --> 00:02:31.124
Your turn. Say: Engagement.

45
00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:35.084
Engagement.

46
00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.597
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.157
Visal, your presentation won first prize. What did you do differently?

48
00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:53.314
I removed most of the text from my slides. What matters most is clarity, not the quantity of information.

49
00:02:54.417 --> 00:02:56.474
But didn't you have a lot of data to share?

50
00:02:57.583 --> 00:03:04.993
Yes, but what the audience remembers is a single key finding, so I built the whole talk around one visual.

51
00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:09.200
And the Q&A? That's what worries most students.

52
00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:17.181
What helped me was predicting the hardest questions and preparing short, honest answers in advance.

53
00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:20.054
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:20.917 --> 00:03:24.031
Grammar focus: Cleft sentences (What is).

55
00:03:24.331 --> 00:03:25.564
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.168
What matters most is clarity.

57
00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:31.954
What the audience remembers is a single key finding.

58
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.546
What helped me was predicting the hardest questions.

59
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:45.700
A 'What' cleft delays the key information to the end, where it receives natural stress. The 'what' clause is singular, so use 'is' or 'was' even if the focus is plural.

60
00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:50.310
Be careful! Don't say: What matters most are clarity.

61
00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:54.580
Say: What matters most is clarity.

62
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.146
A 'what' clause takes a singular verb.

63
00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:00.551
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:02.530
Less Is More.

65
00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:07.756
Researchers often assume that the more data they present, the more impressive they will appear.

66
00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:12.659
Experienced presenters know better: what matters most is clarity.

67
00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:20.252
What audiences typically remember is not a dense table, but a single striking visual and a clearly stated key finding.

68
00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:26.854
Effective speakers therefore design each slide around one idea and rehearse until the delivery feels natural.

69
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:33.361
What distinguishes the best presentations, however, is often the confidence shown during the Q&A.

70
00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:39.142
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:39.983 --> 00:04:42.169
What matters most blank clarity.

72
00:04:42.469 --> 00:04:45.251
A, are. B, is. C, be.

73
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.335
The answer is B: is.

74
00:04:51.483 --> 00:04:55.239
Rewrite with focus: 'The audience remembers one key finding.'

75
00:04:55.539 --> 00:05:03.964
A, What the audience remembers is one key finding.. B, What remembers the audience is one key finding.. C, One key finding is what remembering..

76
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.752
The answer is A: What the audience remembers is one key finding..

77
00:05:12.917 --> 00:05:15.218
What impressed the judges blank her visuals.

78
00:05:15.518 --> 00:05:18.428
A, were. B, was. C, being.

79
00:05:21.967 --> 00:05:23.653
The answer is B: was.

80
00:05:24.817 --> 00:05:28.163
The degree of attention and involvement of an audience is blank.

81
00:05:28.463 --> 00:05:31.815
A, engagement. B, clarity. C, visual.

82
00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.157
The answer is A: engagement.

83
00:05:38.317 --> 00:05:42.894
According to the reading, what often distinguishes the best presentations?

84
00:05:43.194 --> 00:05:49.123
A, confidence during the Q&A. B, the number of slides. C, dense tables.

85
00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:55.804
The answer is A: confidence during the Q&A.

86
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.744
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:01.583 --> 00:06:04.964
What distinguishes a memorable academic presentation?

88
00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:23.157
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:23.457 --> 00:06:59.044
What distinguishes a memorable presentation, in my view, is a clear message that the audience can repeat afterwards. Rather than displaying every result, an effective speaker focuses on one key finding supported by a strong visual. For example, a single well-designed chart can be far more persuasive than ten slides of text. Of course, some technical audiences expect detailed data, so presenters must adapt. Ultimately, what audiences value most is clarity combined with genuine enthusiasm.

90
00:06:59.344 --> 00:07:01.099
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:01.983 --> 00:07:06.447
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and cleft sentences (what is).

92
00:07:06.747 --> 00:07:10.804
Today's value is confidence. Speak up and be proud of who you are.

93
00:07:11.104 --> 00:07:14.279
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:14.579 --> 00:07:17.278
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

